course: everyday
lesson: 455
title: Trusting again
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: After Chloe broke her trust, Mia didn't trust her all at once again. She tested it slowly: first a small secret — «I still sleep with my old teddy». Chloe kept it. A few weeks later, a bigger one. Chloe kept that too. A year later they were best friends again. Trust is built in small steps, like a wall of bricks: one honest act at a time. Saying «trust me» doesn't build it. Being trustworthy again and again does.
explain: Freya had lied about going to the park. She asked her mum, «How long till you trust me again?» Mum said, «As long as it takes you to show me.» For two months Freya was always exactly where she said, and Mum stopped checking. Zoe kept lying about little things — sweets, homework, chores — until her dad said, «I want to believe you, but I can't yet.» That hurt. Zoe started telling the truth even when it was awkward, and after a few months Dad said «I believe you», and Zoe nearly cried. Max had copied homework and owned up; next term his teacher let him mark his own spelling test. «I trust you now.» He marked it honestly — 7 out of 10 — and was prouder of that 7 than of any 10.
explain: In families, the truth comes easier when it's safe. After Jayden lied about a film, his mom in Georgia said: «Next time you mess up, tell me first, you'll get half the trouble.» He tested it: he broke a glass and told her at once. She just said «Thanks for telling me, baby. Grab the broom.» Oisin's da, who'd lied about smoking, said «I won't lie to you any more. If I mess up, I'll tell you.» A month later: «I had one yesterday. I'm sorry.» Oisin hugged him — he trusted his da more because he told the truth when it was hard. Ben and his big brother had blamed each other for years; one day they made a pact: «No more blaming. If one of us does something, he owns it.» The pact mostly held, and Mum said the house was calmer than it had been in years.
explain: Friends can rebuild too. Ella in Auckland admitted she'd made up a fancy holiday. Her friend said «Sweet as» — in New Zealand that means «that's fine» — «I don't care where you go, just be real with me.» They were closer after that. Aroha forgave a friend for a cruel prank, but asked her to say sorry to the whole class too, since they'd all laughed. The friend did, and the class stopped laughing at Aroha. And after Sam lost his game account to a scam, he felt he couldn't trust anyone online. His mum taught him the signs; he started gaming again — carefully — spotted two more scams and reported them. Being tricked once doesn't mean trusting no-one. It means trusting with your eyes open.
explain: At the end of term, Mrs Kelly in Dublin asked her class: «What's the hardest thing about being honest?» Max said, «Saying it first.» Ella said, «Hearing it.» Kai said, «Forgiving it.» «All three,» said Mrs Kelly. «And all three get easier with practice.» Each of them wrote one honest thing on a card and put it in a box.
fact: Trust is rebuilt in small steps: small chances first, bigger ones later.
fact: Trust is earned by actions over time, not by saying «trust me».
fact: Many small lies can use up people's trust, so that even your true words are doubted.
fact: Telling small truths again and again, even awkward ones, is how trust is won back.
fact: When someone trusts you again, protecting that trust feels good and is worth it.
fact: Families where telling the truth is safe tend to have fewer lies.
fact: Admitting a mistake makes a person easier to trust than someone who claims never to make any.
fact: Agreements to own up instead of blaming each other can make a family calmer.
fact: When someone hurts you in front of others, it can be fair to ask for an apology in front of the same people.
fact: Being tricked once does not mean trusting no-one; it means trusting carefully, knowing the signs.
fact: Honesty has three hard parts: saying the truth first, hearing it, and forgiving it; all get easier with practice.
fact: Real friends often forgive small lies and like you more when you are honest.
fact: «Sweet as» (New Zealand, Australia) means «that's fine»; «be real» means be honest; «own it» means admit you did it.
exercise: How did Mia test whether she could trust Chloe again? | With a small secret first, then a bigger one | With her biggest secret straight away, to be sure | By reading all of Chloe's messages | By asking Chloe to swear
exercise: Why is trust compared to a wall of bricks? | It is built one honest act at a time | It is heavy, hard and very tiring to carry | It keeps people out for ever | It falls down in the rain
exercise: What did Freya's mum say about how long trust would take? | As long as it takes you to show me | Exactly one week from today, no longer | Never, you've lost it forever | Until your next birthday
exercise: What did Freya do to win trust back? | Was always exactly where she said | Promised very loudly every day | Gave her mum a present | Stayed in her room for two months
exercise: Why did Zoe's dad say «I can't believe you yet»? | Many small lies had used up his trust | He didn't like Zoe very much any more | He had a bad memory for what she said | He thought she was ill
exercise: How did Zoe earn her dad's trust back? | Telling the truth even when awkward | Promising very loudly never to lie again | Asking her brother to vouch | Doing extra homework
exercise: Why was Max prouder of his 7 than of any 10? | It was honest and showed he'd changed | Seven has always been his lucky number | He copied it from the friend next to him | The teacher gave him a gold sticker for it
exercise: What happened when Jayden told his mom straight away about the glass? | She thanked him and he helped sweep up | She grounded him for a whole month for it | She was furious and shouted | She made him buy a new set of glasses
exercise: Why did Oisin trust his da more after he admitted having a cigarette? | He told the truth when it was hard | He promised never to smoke again, ever | He bought Oisin a present | He blamed one of his mates for it
exercise: What was Ben and his brother's pact? | No more blaming; whoever did it owns it | Always blame the dog | Never speak to each other again till Christmas | Take turns being the one in trouble
exercise: What does «sweet as» mean in New Zealand? | That's fine | That's sugary | That's really rude | That's very fast
exercise: Why did Aroha ask her friend to say sorry to the whole class? | The whole class had laughed at the prank | She wanted her friend to feel embarrassed too | The teacher made a rule about it | Her friend liked giving speeches
exercise: What did Sam learn after being scammed? | To trust carefully, knowing the signs | Never to use the internet again for anything | That all online players are scammers | That scams can't happen twice
exercise: What did Max say is the hardest thing about being honest? | Saying it first | Hearing it back | Forgiving it | Spelling it right
exercise: What did Mrs Kelly say about the three hard parts of honesty? | All three get easier with practice | Only one of them really matters in the end | They never get any easier | Only teachers can do them
exercise: Who is easier to trust? | Someone who admits mistakes | Someone who never admits any | Someone who says «trust me» a lot | Someone who never speaks to anyone
test: Mate after a lie: «just trust me again, I said sorry!» What actually rebuilds trust? | Being honest again and again over time | Saying «trust me» many, many more times | A big expensive present | Waiting one minute
test: Mum: «Tell me first, half the trouble.» Kid smashes a mug and says nothing. What would've happened if he'd told her at once? | Less trouble and more trust | Double trouble for telling | Exactly the same trouble | He'd be grounded for a year
test: Ella's friend: «Sweet as, just be real with me.» How does she feel about Ella's made-up holiday? | She doesn't mind, she just wants honesty | She's furious and won't ever forgive her | She wants to go on the holiday | She thinks the holiday was real
test: Da: «I had one yesterday, son, I'm sorry.» Oisin's mate: «so he broke his promise, he's a liar». What's wiser? | He told the truth when it was hard, so he's easier to trust | He broke a promise, so you can never trust a word he says | He should have hidden it better | Promises don't matter at all
test: Zoe: «why don't you believe me, I'm telling the TRUTH!» Dad: «I can't yet.» What's going on? | Earlier small lies used up his trust | Dad never believes anyone about anything | Dad is just in a bad mood | Zoe is still lying now
test: Sam, after the scam: «I'm never trusting anyone online again, ever». What's a better plan? | Learn the signs and trust carefully | Stay off the internet for life | Trust everyone so it's fair | Only trust people with cool names
test: What would happen if Mia had told Chloe her biggest secret on day one after the fight? | She'd risk being hurt again before trust was rebuilt | Chloe would definitely keep it, because she'd said sorry | Nothing at all could happen | Chloe would tell her a secret back
test: Teacher: «Mark your own spelling test, Max.» What's the teacher showing? | She trusts him again | She is too busy to mark | She thinks he'll cheat | Spelling doesn't matter
test: Big bro: «No more blaming, deal? If I did it, I own it.» What does «own it» mean? | Admit you did it | Buy it from a shop | Keep it as yours forever | Break it all over again
test: In a film, the kid lies once, says sorry once, and everyone trusts him fully that day. What's more true? | Trust usually grows back slowly | Trust always comes back at once | Trust can never come back | One sorry fixes everything
test: A friend laughed at Aroha in front of the class. A quiet sorry by the lockers — enough? | It's fair to ask for a sorry in front of the class | Yes, a quiet sorry is always enough, whatever happened | No sorry is ever enough | Aroha should laugh at her back
exam: Identify the principal means by which trust, once broken, is restored. | Truthful conduct consistently repeated over a period of time | A single emphatic assurance of reliability | The presentation of a valuable gift | The passage of time without any change of conduct
exam: It is given that a parent undertakes to impose only half the usual consequence upon a child who admits a fault immediately. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Such an arrangement tends to make truthful admission more frequent | Such an arrangement invariably encourages further deliberate wrongdoing | Such an arrangement has no effect upon honesty | Such an arrangement is prohibited by custom
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a person who admits his failures? | He is more readily trusted than one who claims to have none | He is less readily trusted than one who conceals them | He is unworthy of any trust whatsoever | He is trusted only by strangers
exam: Determine the conclusion consistent with the established facts concerning a child who has once been deceived online. | He should resume cautiously, having learned the signs of deception | He should never again use any online service | He should trust all online acquaintances equally | He should conceal his experience from adults
